  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nA broad, zonally-oriented ridge of high pressure sites over the \nMid- South region today, with easterly flow along the Gulf Coast \nand our CWA. A weak disturbance in the flow is helping to initiate\nclusters of showers and a few thunderstorms into this afternoon, \nand then drier air and subsidence in its wake will limit precip \nchances for tomorrow. The heat associated with the ridge won't \nbreak, but we should see today's peak heat index values of 105-110\ndegrees nudge lower with the arrival of drier air tomorrow. The \ncurrent Heat Advisory for northeast Louisiana, southeast Arkansas,\nand western/central Mississippi may be the last headline for a \nfew days. \"Limited\" threat conditions for heat stress with peak \nheat index values near or slightly below 105 will continue \ntomorrow and the next few days.\n\nThursday, but especially Friday and Saturday, the weakening \ninfluence of the ridge aloft will permit greater northward spread \nof Gulf moisture and the approach of a surface frontal boundary \nfrom the north. Even if the front itself does not make it into our\nforecast area, storms may develop along and near the front and \nmove through our region or forecast area. Prospects improve for at\nleast scattered afternoon showers and thunderstorms across the \narea over the weekend into early next week. Potential coverage of \nstorms makes heat messaging less certain by late in the weekend, \nso will continue to limit the timing of heat messaging after \nSaturday. /NF/",
